From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chinmoy Dey <chinmoy@nexthop.ai>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Remove mTLS session when its connection is destroyed

bmcweb creates one MutualTLS UserSession in the session store for every
TLS handshake that presents a valid client certificate
(tlsVerifyCallback -> verifyMtlsUser -> generateUserSession). The session
was removed again only in Connection::gracefulClose(), i.e. when the
server decides to close the connection. Every other teardown path left
the session in SessionStore::authTokens:
  - the client closes the connection first (EOF on read -> hardClose())
  - read/write errors and the connection deadline timer (hardClose())
  - every HTTP/2 connection: HTTP2Connection holds the session but never
    removes it
  - WebSocket and SSE upgrades, which take over the socket

The idle timeout did not bound the growth either, because
applySessionTimeouts() only runs from token/cookie logins and from
SessionService reads, which a certificate-only client never triggers.
A client that opens one mTLS connection per request therefore grows the
session table, and bmcweb RSS, by one entry per request for as long as
the process runs (sonic-net/sonic-redfish#44).

Session lifetime
  - sessions.hpp: add bindSessionToConnection(). It returns a pointer to
    the same UserSession that also shares ownership of a
    ConnectionBoundSession; when the last copy of that pointer is
    released the session is removed from the store. The store's own
    reference does not count
  - http_connection.hpp: tlsVerifyCallback() binds the session it gets
    from verifyMtlsUser(). Every holder on the connection side then keeps
    the session listed for exactly as long as it is in use: the HTTP/1
    connection, the HTTP/2 connection it upgrades to, a WebSocket
    connection (which stores req.session), an SSE connection and requests
    still being handled. No per-path hand-off is needed, and a failed
    upgrade cannot strand the session. Re-assigning in
    tlsVerifyCallback() releases a session from an earlier verification
  - server_sent_event_impl.hpp: keep req.session for the life of the
    event stream; it kept no reference, so the session of a live stream
    would have left the store
  - sessions.hpp: applySessionTimeouts() skips MutualTLS sessions.
    lastUpdated is never refreshed for them, so the sweep expired the
    session of a connection that was still open and authorized, hiding
    it from SessionService and from removeSessionsByUsername()
  - sessions.hpp: run applySessionTimeouts() in generateUserSession() so
    token sessions are expired even if no token login or SessionService
    read ever happens, and do not let generateUserSession() clear the
    needWrite that the sweep just set

Idle connections
Neither HTTP version bounded an idle connection, and bmcweb sets no TCP
keepalive, so a peer that goes silent or disappears without closing the
socket held the connection, its file descriptor and its mTLS session
until bmcweb restarted.
  - http_connection.hpp: the 15 second deadline covers the handshake and
    an in-progress read or write, but afterDoWrite() cancels it and the
    keep-alive path waits for the next request header with no timer.
    Arm a 60 second deadline there
  - http2_connection.hpp: add an idle timer, re-armed on every completed
    read and write. After 60 seconds without traffic the connection gets
    a GOAWAY and is closed. While a request stream is open the timer is
    re-armed instead, so a slow handler is not cut off, but only for 5
    periods: a stream is opened by the first HEADERS frame, so an open
    stream alone cannot keep a silent connection forever
  - http2_connection.hpp: the timer handler re-checks expiry(), so a
    handler queued just before the timer was re-armed does not close a
    connection that saw traffic
  - http2_connection.hpp: the completion handler captured a raw `this`.
    Capture a weak_ptr, since the connection can now be closed while a
    handler is still running (this was already reachable on peer EOF)
  - nghttp2_adapters.hpp: add terminateSession() for the GOAWAY

Signed-off-by: Chinmoy Dey <chinmoy@nexthop.ai>
